# Round XXIX Track A: MEMORY-UNSAFE class BEFORE FIX, REJECT AFTER FIX.
# `[]` was not gated on the `Index[K,V]` trait, so indexing a type that
# implements NO indexing trait was silently accepted and lowered as an
# UNCHECKED RAW OFFSET READ on BOTH backends.
#
# PRE-FIX (measured 2026-08-03 at HEAD before this round with
# `print(f"{p[0]} {p[1]} {p[5]}")` — the f-string form the original
# repro used):
#   C    : 11 22 281474539212408
#   LLVM : 11 22 281473722332240
#
# So p[0]/p[1] read fields a/b by offset, and p[5] read OUT OF BOUNDS
# past the struct (the value is ASLR-dependent; the stable fact is that
# it is an unchecked read of memory the program does not own).
#
# ROOT — and this is a CLASS, not an instance. `Index[K,V]` (`V get(self, K)`)
# and `IndexMut[K,V]` exist as builtin traits (`src/semantic/traits.rs:754`,
# `:765`), but the `[]` operator never consulted them as a gate. The other
# costumes of the SAME defect (Set/HashSet/Tuple/Option/enum/primitive int)
# are all closed by the operator-level gate in this round — Core #4.
#
# POST-FIX: check-time rejection with `E_NotIndexable` naming the missing
# `Index` impl. `Pair` must `equip Pair with Index[int, int]` to opt in;
# other receivers get the same trait-registry answer through one path
# (Layering rule 3: `Index[K,V]` is the typed metadata, `has_trait_impl_by_name`
# is the ONE consultation site).
#
# ⚠ PROBE DISCIPLINE (AGENTS.md Core #15(d) / "MIND THE PROBE"): this
# fixture asserts the reject with `int x = p[0]` — NEVER `print(f"{p[0]}")`,
# because interpolation discards typecheck errors on its operands and a
# rejected call reads as accepted (the exact trap that hid the CoW `&`
# class for 5 months). Verified 2026-08-03: with THIS fix landed,
# `print(f"{p[0]}")` still returns "OK: no semantic errors" — the f-string
# swallows the reject. The bare-expression form below is the honest probe.
struct Pair:
    int a
    int b

int main():
    Pair p = Pair(11, 22)
    int x = p[0]
    int y = p[5]
    print(x)
    print(y)
    return 0
